The directory is empty — there's no existing project. Here's the implementation pattern you need. I'll create a working example showing exactly how to wire it up.

**Two things to add to your project:**

### 1. Create `Middleware/ApiExceptionHandler.cs`

```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Diagnostics;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;

namespace YourApp.Middleware;

internal sealed class ApiExceptionHandler(ILogger<ApiExceptionHandler> logger)
    : IExceptionHandler
{
    public async ValueTask<bool> TryHandleAsync(
        HttpContext httpContext,
        Exception exception,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken)
    {
        var (statusCode, title) = exception switch
        {
            KeyNotFoundException => (StatusCodes.Status404NotFound, "Not Found"),
            ArgumentException   => (StatusCodes.Status400BadRequest, "Bad Request"),
            InvalidOperationException => (StatusCodes.Status409Conflict, "Conflict"),
            _ => (0, (string?)null)
        };

        if (statusCode == 0)
            return false; // fall through to default handler (returns 500)

        logger.LogWarning(exception, "Handled API exception: {Title}", title);

        httpContext.Response.StatusCode = statusCode;
        await httpContext.Response.WriteAsJsonAsync(new ProblemDetails
        {
            Status = statusCode,
            Title = title,
            Detail = title,           // safe — never use exception.Message
            Instance = httpContext.Request.Path
        }, cancellationToken);

        return true;
    }
}
```

### 2. Register in `Program.cs`

```csharp
// ── Services ──
builder.Services.AddExceptionHandler<ApiExceptionHandler>();
builder.Services.AddProblemDetails();  // formats unhandled (500) as Problem Details too

// ── Middleware (order matters — place before routing) ──
app.UseExceptionHandler();   // invokes ApiExceptionHandler, then default
app.UseStatusCodePages();    // converts bare status codes (e.g. 404) to Problem Details
```

### How it works

| Exception thrown anywhere | HTTP response |
|---|---|
| `KeyNotFoundException` | `404` Problem Details |
| `ArgumentException` | `400` Problem Details |
| `InvalidOperationException` | `409` Problem Details |
| Anything else | `500` Problem Details (no sensitive details) |

- `AddProblemDetails()` ensures the **default** handler also returns RFC 7807 JSON for unmatched exceptions (status 500), with no stack trace or message leakage.
- `UseStatusCodePages()` catches endpoints that set a 4xx/5xx status code without a body and wraps them in Problem Details too.
- No try-catch needed in any endpoint — the middleware pipeline handles everything globally.
